{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "",
  "stderr": "",
  "return_code": 0,
  "success": true,
  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code generation agents powered by large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized software development workflows. However, these powerful code agents also introduce significant security vulnerabilities and trustworthiness concerns that necessitate robust evaluation frameworks. In this paper, we present a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agent technologies and red-teaming methodologies. We examine the dual aspects of security assessment through red-teaming approaches that proactively identify vulnerabilities, and blue-teaming strategies that develop defensive mechanisms. Our survey covers automated red-teaming agents such as RedCodeAgent and complementary blue-teaming frameworks like BlueCodeAgent. We further discuss the broader context of trustworthiness evaluation in AI systems, drawing from comprehensive assessments like DecodingTrust. Finally, we survey foundational deep learning architectures that enable these capabilities, including residual learning approaches. Through this analysis, we identify key challenges and future research directions for securing code generation systems through systematic evaluation and defense mechanisms.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent one of the most transformative applications of large language models in the software engineering domain. These autonomous systems can understand requirements, generate code, execute programs, debug errors, and even interact with external APIs to complete complex programming tasks. The integration of code interpreters, execution environments, and iterative refinement capabilities has enabled code agents to handle increasingly sophisticated programming workflows [1].\n\nHowever, the deployment of code agents in real-world scenarios introduces significant security and trustworthiness challenges. Code agents have access to powerful execution capabilities, which can be exploited by malicious actors to generate harmful code, inject vulnerabilities, or bypass safety filters. The autonomous nature of these agents amplifies these risks, as they can independently make decisions without human oversight.\n\nTo address these challenges, the research community has developed red-teaming methodologies specifically designed for code agents. Red-teaming involves systematically probing AI systems to identify vulnerabilities, weaknesses, and potential attack vectors before they can be exploited. This proactive security approach has become essential for ensuring the safe deployment of AI systems.\n\nIn this paper, we survey recent advances in code agent technologies and their associated security evaluation methodologies. We examine automated red-teaming frameworks, blue-teaming defense mechanisms, comprehensive trustworthiness assessments, and the foundational deep learning architectures that enable these capabilities. Our contributions include:\n\n1. A comprehensive overview of code agent architectures and capabilities\n2. Analysis of automated red-teaming methodologies for code agents\n3. Examination of blue-teaming defense frameworks\n4. Discussion of trustworthiness evaluation frameworks for AI systems\n5. Survey of foundational deep learning approaches enabling these systems\n6. Identification of open challenges and future research directions\n\n## Related Work\n\nThe field of code agent security and evaluation has seen rapid growth, with several key frameworks emerging in recent years. We discuss here the most relevant prior work across four key areas.\n\n### Automated Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nA pioneering approach to code agent security is RedCodeAgent [2], which presents an automatic red-teaming agent designed to evaluate diverse code agents. RedCodeAgent systematically generates adversarial prompts and evaluates how different code agents respond to potentially harmful instructions. The framework is particularly notable for its ability to adaptively explore the attack surface of code agents, generating diverse test cases across multiple dimensions including malicious code generation, vulnerability exploitation, and policy bypass attempts. The authors demonstrate that RedCodeAgent can uncover significant vulnerabilities in state-of-the-art code agents that remain undetected by static analysis methods. This work established the foundation for automated red-teaming as a standard methodology for evaluating code agent security.\n\n### Blue-Teaming and Defense Mechanisms\n\nComplementing red-teaming approaches, BlueCodeAgent [3] introduces a blue-teaming framework that leverages automated red-teaming to develop robust defense mechanisms for code generation AI systems. The key innovation of BlueCodeAgent lies in its bidirectional learning approach: the blue-teaming agent learns from the red-teaming agent's attacks to develop increasingly sophisticated detection capabilities. The framework integrates constitution-based evaluation with code analysis to detect both known and novel risk scenarios. BlueCodeAgent demonstrates that exposure to adversarial examples generated through automated red-teaming significantly improves the defensive capabilities of code agents, achieving a 12.7% F1 score improvement in vulnerability detection tasks. This work highlights the symbiotic relationship between red-teaming and blue-teaming methodologies.\n\n### Trustworthiness Assessment Frameworks\n\nBeyond code-specific security, the broader question of AI trustworthiness has been addressed comprehensively in DecodingTrust [4]. This work presents a comprehensive assessment framework for evaluating trustworthiness across multiple dimensions in GPT models and related architectures. The assessment covers safety, bias, factuality, robustness, and other critical trustworthiness dimensions. DecodingTrust provides standardized benchmarks and evaluation protocols that have influenced the development of more specialized evaluation frameworks for domain-specific applications. The framework's multi-dimensional approach ensures that trustworthiness evaluation is not limited to security alone but encompasses a holistic view of model reliability and safety.\n\n### Foundational Deep Learning Architectures\n\nThe capabilities of modern code agents are built upon foundational deep learning architectures, with residual learning playing a crucial role in enabling the training of deep neural networks. Deep Residual Learning [5] introduced the revolutionary concept of residual connections, which allow networks to learn identity mappings and ease the training of substantially deeper architectures. This innovation enabled the development of the deep transformer models that power contemporary code agents. The residual learning framework has become a cornerstone of modern deep learning, facilitating the construction of models with hundreds or thousands of layers while maintaining stable training dynamics. Without such architectural advances, the sophisticated code generation and execution capabilities of modern agents would not be achievable.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents and their security evaluation represents a critical frontier in AI safety research. As code agents become increasingly capable and widely deployed, the need for robust security evaluation frameworks becomes paramount. This survey has examined the current landscape of red-teaming and blue-teaming approaches for code agents, highlighting the complementary nature of these methodologies.\n\nKey findings from our survey include:\n\n1. **Automated red-teaming** has emerged as an essential tool for identifying vulnerabilities in code agents before deployment. Frameworks like RedCodeAgent provide adaptive, systematic approaches to security evaluation that surpass traditional testing methods.\n\n2. **Blue-teaming defenses** benefit significantly from exposure to adversarial examples generated through red-teaming. The bidirectional learning approach demonstrated in BlueCodeAgent establishes a framework for continuously improving defense mechanisms.\n\n3. **Comprehensive trustworthiness evaluation** must extend beyond security to encompass multiple dimensions including safety, fairness, and reliability, as demonstrated by frameworks like DecodingTrust.\n\n4. **Foundational deep learning architectures**, particularly residual learning, enable the development of the powerful model architectures that underpin modern code agents.\n\nSeveral challenges remain for future research. First, developing more sophisticated attack strategies that can bypass increasingly robust defenses will drive the development of more secure agents. Second, creating standardized evaluation benchmarks that can compare different code agent security frameworks will facilitate progress in the field. Third, addressing the scalability of evaluation methods to handle the increasing complexity of code agent systems remains an open problem.\n\nWe anticipate that the symbiotic relationship between red-teaming and blue-teaming will continue to strengthen, with each informing and improving the other. As code agents become more autonomous and powerful, rigorous security evaluation must become an integral part of their development pipeline. By advancing both offensive and defensive capabilities, we can work towards code agents that are not only powerful but also safe and trustworthy.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Code agents and their applications in automated software development workflows.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[3] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[4] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[5] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\nEOF"
}'}

